                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "This is a review spanning all of the Millennium series (games 1-5). After about 10 years of playing, I've finally finished this series. I had first intended to 100% all of the achievements, but I only managed to with the first 3 games. Partially, this is due to bad achievement design, as there are many opportunities to unfortunately lock yourself out of most achievements early on, and some require multiple playthroughs, many which rely on Hard Mode and really don't bring much more challenge other than fighting bosses that are basically damage sponges with unfair sweeping statuses that you can't heal from easily. (The amount of times I died to total party paralysis was laughable.)\n\nBut, even bearing that in mind, I still really enjoyed this game series. I first played it as a tester when I used to work at Big Fish Games and I just really enjoyed the story itself (classic underdog story) and all of the characters. It wasn't until later when I got all of the games that I decided I wanted to finish the series, more as a personal goal than anything else. With the right polish, this could have been an amazing RPG Maker game series - comparing to the Aveyond series - however I don't think it quite lives up to that. Regardless, the creativity on display from how the game is presented and how you move through the world is worth playing just for the experience alone. Some great ideas were in here!\n\nIt mostly suffers from too many characters and not enough screen-time to develop them all fully. There are several that probably could have been combined into one character or just removed and the story wouldn't have suffered for it. Mostly, I think after 5 games I wanted a little more conclusion to the story in terms of a final cutscene. We could have benefited from seeing all of the characters' home towns enriched over time. I really wanted a companion scene to the first game since it's apparently being told as a sort of bedtime story.\n\nThe most frustrating part of the game was the final showdown, which takes place in Game 5. No matter how much preparation you do for the final battle, it still comes down to a matter of the flip of a coin for many battles and it's not really clear how to defeat most characters - even with the included Strategy Guide, which I did purchase. You can still do everything right and still lose. Thankfully, it does provide plenty of save points, so it's not completely impossible to win. \n\nThe strategy guide for Millennium 5 also suffered from a lack of clear direction (there's so much back-tracking it gets hard to follow and easy to get lost, making me flip back and forth through dozens of pages in my PDF file) and it's not always clear how to enter (and more importantly escape!) from the Secret Rooms.\n\nWhat I will say is that the unique gameplay is where this game really shines. The game became much more enjoyable in the later games where most of your exploration was done vertically and horizontally through simple platforming puzzles. The only downside to that being if you didn't have the Air Sandals equipped, you could potentially soft-lock yourself or get lost at best. But it really added character to this game and made the areas come alive. I honestly wish the whole game had been designed in a platformer perspective, because I think that would make it stand out from many other games. I also appreciate the sheer creativity in the locations, particularly the ancient temples, which were always fun to explore.\n\nExploration in this game was also very much rewarded handsomely. I loved that you could find hidden items to not only boost all your stats but your money and general experience for levels as well. Honestly, the only thing it really suffered from in this area was not offering better rewards for finding these places. If I'm going out of my way to find these, I want them to be worth the effort.\n\nThis game was made over a decade ago, so much of the language in the game has not aged gracefully at all. Character dialogues often run on, get cut off, or just appear to not have been edited fully. Some scenes feel too short and some feel way too long. Regardless, the story itself really does drive you forward and you just want to see Marine succeed, so there's something to be said for that.\n\nOverall, would I recommend this to everyone? No, this series really does appeal to a certain type of player who really enjoys min-maxing builds, doing perfect runs, and seeing how fast they can go. It doesn't appeal to a more casual player, which is unfortunate. Know that if you get into this series, you're getting into it for the long run. I believe I logged about 500 hours total across all the games - and that's not going for 100% completion on the last two, which probably would have added about another 25-50 hours. Ultimately, I have too many other games to play to drive my playthroughs to perfection, but I am glad that I spent some time in Myst. I learned a lot about RPG game design from playing this game and I can honestly say my time was well spent.",
